                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{4}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWrite down the integral that gives the surface area of the shell.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Surface area $\\,=4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $x^2\\sqrt{1+16x^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}x.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nWrite down the missing parts of a parametrization of our shell below.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $r(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x^2\\sin(\\theta)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩,\\,x\\in [0,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the norm of the fundamental vector product for the surface $r(x,\\theta ),$ found in the previous step.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387713 1113 #uuid "96309fb9-4aab-592a-923c-5986fddbf65e" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387713 1145 "Recall that for a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn part 4, we got the surface\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂\\theta }(x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂\\theta }(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂\\theta }(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{x}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +8{x}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{x}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{x}^{3},\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding norm is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(8{x}^{3})^{2}+(-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{x}^{6}+4{x}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{x}^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{x}^{6}+4{x}^{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{x}^{6}+4{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387715 13194139536184 true] [17592186387714 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536184 true] [17592186387714 1041 "field-1" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387714 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387715 13194139536184 true] [17592186387714 1110 #uuid "f4b50361-3bae-55ee-ac68-63c9c31a1e01" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387715 1034 "2x^2\\sqrt{1+16x^2}" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387715 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536184 true] [17592186387715 1111 #uuid "d55e640c-63ea-5deb-be1a-610bdf26fc7d" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387716 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387717 13194139536184 true] [17592186387716 1089 "q-167649" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387716 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536184 true] [17592186387716 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/assets/22a909d34b63517bf5ccf90560a8070d20e1aa91a6c9eaf8f4e5326208b9d751.png)\n\n \n\nThe shell above is obtained by rotating the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis, as shown above. For each point $(x,y,z)$ on the shell, the charge density (electric charge per unit surface area) of the shell is given by the function \n\n$$\nd(x,y,z)=\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the curvature $κ(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at $x=0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n   