n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=4x,\\,{y}^{″}(x)=4\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|4|}{{[1+{(4x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{(1+16{x}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{4}{{(1+16(0)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{1}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387721 13194139536184 true] [17592186387720 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536184 true] [17592186387720 1041 "field-1" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387720 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387721 13194139536184 true] [17592186387720 1110 #uuid "6fa45bb0-776b-5af4-bb92-4c3356549c2f" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387721 1034 "4" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387721 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536184 true] [17592186387721 1111 #uuid "b7bdd6ef-ff65-5347-acf7-991e42d1a1cb" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387722 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387723 13194139536184 true] [17592186387722 1089 "q-167651" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387722 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536184 true] [17592186387722 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/assets/22a909d34b63517bf5ccf90560a8070d20e1aa91a6c9eaf8f4e5326208b9d751.png)\n\n \n\nThe shell above is obtained by rotating the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis, as shown above. For each point $(x,y,z)$ on the shell, the charge density (electric charge per unit surface area) of the shell is given by the function \n\n$$\nd(x,y,z)=\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the curvature $κ(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at $x=0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $κ(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWhat is the radius of curvature $R(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $R(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{4}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWrite down the integral that gives the surface area of the shell.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Surface area $\\,=4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}x.$" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387722 1113 #uuid "0ddcfbbb-2a58-53d2-94ee-9f84023cfdc6" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387722 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})=4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387723 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387724 13194139536184 true] [17592186387723 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536184 true] [17592186387723 1041 "field-1" 13194139536184 true] [17592186387723 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387724 13194139536184 true] [17592186387723 1110 #uuid "58b61a96-d54f-5c